146
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:33,720
And if they're shopping for a different kind of dazzle,

147
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:37,460
three decked out semis flaunt the trucker's delight.

148
00:07:37,461 --> 00:07:41,540
Chrome, that protective shiny metal that makes rigs glisten

149
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:43,340
in the sun.

150
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:45,500
We've added a lot of chrome accessories to these show

151
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:46,540
trucks.

152
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:50,640
We have full fenders to a step, six-inch stacks, and, of course,

153
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:53,440
the custom front bumper.

154
00:07:53,540 --> 00:07:57,340
Chrome is slang for chromium, a highly reflective blue-white

155
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:01,080
metal resistant to tarnish and corrosion.

156
00:08:01,180 --> 00:08:03,180
It's added to other metals, like aluminum,

157
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:05,580
to form a protective and attractive covering

158
00:08:05,581 --> 00:08:07,580
through a process called electroplating.

159
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:13,580
First, the aluminum part is wired to the negative pole

160
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:18,420
of a battery and chromium to the positive pole.

161
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:21,300
Next, both metals are immersed in a solution of chromic

162
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:26,440
and sulfuric acid to permit the flow of electricity.

163
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:28,640
Since the aluminum is negatively charged,

164
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:30,840
it attracts the positively charged chromium.

165
00:08:30,841 --> 00:08:34,740
chromium.

166
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:38,240
Automakers in the 1950s began using chrome to produce cars

167
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:41,580
with a flashier appeal.

168
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:45,180
Soaring tail fins and grinning wide-mouthed grills

169
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:47,080
lined the highways.

170
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:51,400
And chrome became a household word.

171
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:55,540
Today, chrome has taken a back seat in passenger car design.

172
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:58,240
But modern big rigs carry on its glistening legacy.

173
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:05,280
After dishing out their dough on a sparkling chrome accessory,

174
00:09:05,380 --> 00:09:07,480
drivers can retire to the truckers area.

175
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:15,180
It's home to 23 private shower rooms, a driver's den,

176
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:19,420
a 60-seat Dolby surround sound movie theater,

177
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:21,560
and a barbershop.

178
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:23,700
There's even a dentist.

179
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:26,360
If there is such a thing called an emergency room

180
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:28,600
of dental care, this would be it.

181
00:09:28,601 --> 00:09:31,000
I'm usually the truck driver's best friend

182
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:33,700
because I am taking them out of pain.

183
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:35,840
Although perks like a dentist and a barber

184
00:09:35,940 --> 00:09:38,980
may entice a driver to pull into this mega pit stop,

185
00:09:39,080 --> 00:09:42,740
a home-cooked meal can really draw a crowd.

186
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:46,260
The restaurant's the heartbeat of the truck stop.

187
00:09:46,360 --> 00:09:48,980
Drivers like comfort food, and they like large portions,

188
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:50,260
and they want to feel like they're

189
00:09:50,360 --> 00:09:53,160
getting a lot for their money.

190
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:55,220
The Iowa 80 Kitchen Restaurant

191
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:57,560
serves more than a million cups of coffee.

192
00:09:57,561 --> 00:10:02,560
2 million eggs, and 90 tons of meat each year.

193
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:05,500
When you can find a truck stop that's got homemade food,

194
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:07,200
boy, you live for that.

195
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:10,900
I'm going to live high on the hog today.

196
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,940
This type of roadside pit stop dates back to the 1920s.

197
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:18,980
As cars and trucks began dominating America's roads,

198
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:22,760
the original mom and pop truck stops sprouted up.

199
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:27,560
They catered to truck drivers by providing just the basics.

200
00:10:27,561 --> 00:10:31,560
fuel and a mattress. They'd have bunk rooms,

201
00:10:31,660 --> 00:10:35,560
basically a spare room in the back of the station where people

202
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:38,900
would be pretty crammed in, but it was a place to rest for the

203
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,900
night. The trucking industry

204
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:44,140
had gotten a jump start during World War I, fueled by America's

205
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:49,680
need for efficient transport. As military traffic clogged the

206
00:10:49,780 --> 00:10:52,620
nation's rail lines, trucks started to crowd the roads to

207
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:55,960
make shorter hauls. You had thousands of trained

208
00:10:55,961 --> 00:11:00,200
drivers, as well as thousands of vehicles that were created for

209
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:01,300
the war effort.

210
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:06,440
So the industry really took hold and expanded rapidly.

211
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:10,640
By 1935, about 40% of all communities

212
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:14,340
were dependent upon truck service.

213
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:16,180
More mom-and-pop truck stops started

214
00:11:16,280 --> 00:11:19,220
dotting America's local highways.

215
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:22,520
But by the end of World War II, these small operations

216
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:25,960
were primed to be supersized.

217
00:11:25,961 --> 00:11:29,000
The return of the nation's GIs and the post-war building

218
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:32,000
boom made it clear that the US highway system could never

219
00:11:32,100 --> 00:11:35,340
effectively absorb truck traffic stretching from coast to coast.

220
00:11:38,440 --> 00:11:41,180
Though more than 200 highways crisscrossed the United States

221
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:45,020
in the 1950s, they were jammed with vehicles

222
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:47,840
and inadequately designed for the era's faster and wider

223
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:50,320
trucks.

224
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:55,020
The Federal Interstate Highway Act of 1956 changed that by

225
00:11:55,021 --> 00:11:58,060
the creation of a national system of superhighways that

226
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:01,520
promised a quicker means of moving goods.

227
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:04,860
The launching of the interstate system really

228
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:07,260
resulted in some mega stops.

229
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:10,260
Truck stops no longer became something feasible for an

230
00:12:10,360 --> 00:12:13,580
independent owner to construct and run.

231
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:17,880
It was when the oil companies really got into the game big

232
00:12:17,980 --> 00:12:18,680
time.

233
00:12:21,180 --> 00:12:23,380
When Standard Oil opened Iowa 80 Truck

234
00:12:23,381 --> 00:12:28,880
truck stop in 1964, it sat on roughly five acres.

235
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:33,120
Since then, its owners have developed more than 75 acres,

236
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:36,920
reserving plenty of room to expand.

237
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:41,140
But there's more to an ultimate truck stop than size.

238
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:44,360
How about a high-tech hookup that not only makes a trucker's

239
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:47,500
life a breeze, but also could save more than a billion

240
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:51,380
gallons of diesel each year?

241
00:12:51,381 --> 00:12:53,340
The average truck stop in the United States

242
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:58,480
takes in about $7.8 million in revenue each year.

243
00:12:58,580 --> 00:13:00,920
Truck stops will return on Modern Marvels.

244
00:13:06,020 --> 00:13:08,140
The truck stop at night.

245
00:13:08,240 --> 00:13:10,740
From a distance, it seems peaceful and serene.

246
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:20,740
But up close, it's a deafening hum of idling engines.

247
00:13:20,741 --> 00:13:23,380
Truckers idle their engines for various reasons,

248
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:25,380
including keeping the heat on in the winter,

249
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:27,480
the air conditioning on in the summer,

250
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:30,080
powering their laptops, their DVD players,

251
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:33,080
charging up their cell phones.

252
00:13:33,180 --> 00:13:36,480
Idling trucks burn about a gallon of diesel every hour,

253
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:40,600
and America's army of truckers idle away 1.7 billion gallons

254
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:43,600
of diesel every year.

255
00:13:43,700 --> 00:13:46,400
Idling also adds wear and tear to the engine,

256
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:50,740
and not surprisingly, dumps pollutants into the atmosphere.

257
00:13:50,741 --> 00:13:54,640
One company thinks it has a better idea.

258
00:13:54,740 --> 00:13:57,340
Idle Air allows truckers to shut off their engines

259
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:00,780
while still powering up their toys.

260
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:03,880
Idle Air is advanced truck stop electrification.

261
00:14:03,980 --> 00:14:06,980
Basically, what we've done is devised a way to deliver

262
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:11,600
all the creature comfort services to a parked truck.

263
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:14,840
At the Petro Travel Stop in Knoxville, Tennessee,

264
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:19,540
114 trucks can plug into Idle Air.

265
00:14:19,541 --> 00:14:23,040
All drivers need is a $10 window adapter

266
00:14:23,140 --> 00:14:25,380
to connect to the system.

267
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:27,780
One of my favorite things about using the idle air

268
00:14:27,880 --> 00:14:31,020
is that it enables me to turn the engine off at night

269
00:14:31,120 --> 00:14:33,060
so that I don't have to listen to that rumbling

270
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:34,560
and the constant vibration in the truck.

271
00:14:34,660 --> 00:14:36,060
You sleep a lot better.

272
00:14:36,160 --> 00:14:39,500
I typically spend two to three months at a time

273
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:40,800
out on the road.

274
00:14:40,900 --> 00:14:43,060
So the idle air to me with the internet access

275
00:14:43,160 --> 00:14:48,000
and the phone hookup on it allows me to keep in touch at home.

276
00:14:48,001 --> 00:14:51,400
The system also offers games, movies on demand,

277
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:54,700
and more than 60 TV channels, so drivers can keep up

278
00:14:54,800 --> 00:14:55,700
with their favorite shows.

279
00:14:58,840 --> 00:15:02,040
But there's more to the system than drive-in entertainment.

280
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:04,620
There's also a heater air conditioner.

281
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:07,280
Every idle air unit sits above a parking space

282
00:15:07,380 --> 00:15:10,720
along a truss or support structure.

283
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:14,120
A hose connects each unit to a service delivery module

284
00:15:14,220 --> 00:15:18,000
that provides individual electrical service.

285
00:15:18,001 --> 00:15:20,824
Idle Air's inventor, A.C.

286
00:15:20,825 --> 00:15:28,280
Wilson, came up with his initial concept in 1999 while vacationing

287
00:15:28,281 --> 00:15:32,460
with his brother-in-law, a long-haul trucker who just received a ticket for idling.

288
00:15:33,540 --> 00:15:36,460
I went to bed that night thinking more about it.

289
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:38,940
You know, how can I help these truckers?

290
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:45,060
At 4.30 in the morning, I woke up and I said, here I am in my motorhome.

291
00:15:45,061 --> 00:15:47,260
I'm just as comfortable as a bug in a rug.

292
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,540
Why can't I do that for the trucker?

293
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:55,240
Wilson went to work and created his first prototype,

294
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:59,140
a surface-mounted unit with a hose that ran up into the window,

295
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:02,040
providing climate control and power.

296
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:04,760
We quickly saw that in order to package these services

297
00:16:04,860 --> 00:16:07,560
together, we would need to do it overhead so that we could

298
00:16:07,660 --> 00:16:10,760
distribute these systems safely.

299
00:16:10,860 --> 00:16:12,500
Engineers took Wilson's brainchild

300
00:16:12,600 --> 00:16:16,500
one step further, developing an advanced delivery system.

301
00:16:16,501 --> 00:16:18,800
This duct set is a concentric duct.

302
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:21,440
Basically, the supply air will be delivered to the driver

303
00:16:21,540 --> 00:16:24,280
through this center tube, whereas the return air

304
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:27,440
will pass through here and then past our advanced filtration

305
00:16:27,540 --> 00:16:29,540
technology.

306
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:32,280
At an hourly rate of $1.85, the system

307
00:16:32,380 --> 00:16:37,760
saves about $1 an hour in fuel, plus wear and tear on the engine.

308
00:16:37,860 --> 00:16:40,120
And it greatly reduces all those greenhouse gases

309
00:16:40,220 --> 00:16:43,460
floating skyward.

310
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:46,000
The Weidel Air services more than 100 locations

311
00:16:46,001 --> 00:16:47,400
in 30 states.

312
00:16:47,500 --> 00:16:53,300
That's still only about 2% of all truck stops.

313
00:16:53,400 --> 00:16:55,080
Truck stop electrification programs

314
00:16:55,180 --> 00:16:57,980
are hard to find for the average truck driver today.

315
00:16:58,080 --> 00:16:59,820
So therefore, most truck drivers,

316
00:16:59,920 --> 00:17:03,180
a lot of truck drivers are going with generators or APUs

317
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:07,960
to provide the power that they need for their sleepers.

318
00:17:08,060 --> 00:17:10,120
An APU, or auxiliary power unit,

319
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:13,500
is a small motor fueled by the same diesel that runs the engine

320
00:17:13,600 --> 00:17:16,000
in the tractor.

321
00:17:16,001 --> 00:17:17,560
If you idle one of these big engines,

322
00:17:17,660 --> 00:17:21,100
you're going to use up to a gallon of fuel an hour.

323
00:17:21,200 --> 00:17:24,980
With an APU, you're going to be maybe one quart an hour,

324
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:27,540
and you will achieve the same thing.

325
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:29,380
These portable power systems installed

326
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:32,120
right onto the truck have enabled some rigs

327
00:17:32,220 --> 00:17:35,460
to become downright lavish.

328
00:17:35,560 --> 00:17:40,160
My favorite thing in this truck is my fireplace.

329
00:17:40,260 --> 00:17:43,200
In the cold winters, it's just so cozy,

330
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:44,660
and I love to read.

331
00:17:44,661 --> 00:17:46,060
It keeps you nice and warm.

332
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:47,560
It just feels like you're at home.

333
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:52,180
Truckers have traveled a long, hard road,

334
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:54,100
getting to a luxury sleeper like this one.

335
00:17:55,880 --> 00:17:57,720
Before trucks had room for beds,

336
00:17:58,260 --> 00:18:00,380
drivers had to literally sleep where they worked.

337
00:18:01,140 --> 00:18:03,400
The driver really had nothing available to him.

338
00:18:04,020 --> 00:18:06,680
Laying a board across both seats in the early days

339
00:18:06,681 --> 00:18:10,180
and sleeping on that was fairly common practice.

340
00:18:10,181 --> 00:18:15,180
In 1917, Goodyear introduced the first sleeper cab.

341
00:18:15,280 --> 00:18:16,820
They were very small at first.

342
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:18,920
Basically, it was just a bed.

343
00:18:19,020 --> 00:18:22,520
And as the years go by, the manufacturing of truck

344
00:18:22,620 --> 00:18:25,420
companies started making bigger and bigger sleepers.

345
00:18:25,520 --> 00:18:28,340
You know, basically, just a place for a TV and a bigger

346
00:18:28,440 --> 00:18:30,940
bed, some storage.

347
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:33,140
Today, almost all long-haul drivers

348
00:18:33,240 --> 00:18:35,340
spend the night at truck stops tucked

349
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:36,840
in their very own sleeper.

350
00:18:36,841 --> 00:18:41,380
sleeper. Some drivers have taken their

351
00:18:41,381 --> 00:18:45,340
sleepers to the extreme with custom upscale compartments,

352
00:18:45,341 --> 00:18:48,880
like this big bunk, manufactured at ARI Legacy

353
00:18:48,881 --> 00:18:51,080
Sleepers in Shipshawana, Indiana.

354
00:18:54,800 --> 00:18:57,500
These custom sleepers come fully equipped with a space

355
00:18:57,501 --> 00:19:00,840
saving shower and toilet combo.

356
00:19:00,841 --> 00:19:05,000
Talk about multitasking. I love the toilet in the truck

357
00:19:05,001 --> 00:19:07,900
because then I don't have to go to rest areas.

358
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
And I love to shower.

359
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:12,400
I have about a 40-gallon tank.

360
00:19:12,500 --> 00:19:14,740
And so I can have one every day.

361
00:19:14,840 --> 00:19:19,340
This here has an on and off switch.

362
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:21,980
So you, while you're washing your hair,

363
00:19:22,080 --> 00:19:26,760
you can turn the water off, but yet keep the temperature

364
00:19:26,860 --> 00:19:28,360
of the water.

365
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:29,800
Having your own private restroom

366
00:19:29,900 --> 00:19:32,660
isn't the only perk in these digs.

367
00:19:32,760 --> 00:19:34,760
We also have what we call the bed dinette.

368
00:19:34,761 --> 00:19:38,760
that just folds back down and you have a 48-inch mattress to sleep on.

369
00:19:38,761 --> 00:19:42,760
We have a sink with solid surface countertop, hot and cold running water.

370
00:19:42,761 --> 00:19:45,760
We also have an electric two burner stove in this unit.

371
00:19:45,761 --> 00:19:50,760
And over here we have a refrigerator and freezer with a microwave convection oven.

372
00:19:50,761 --> 00:19:52,760
You can actually bake cookies in this.

373
00:19:52,761 --> 00:19:58,760
We also have a 26-inch LCD widescreen TV with surround sound.

374
00:19:58,761 --> 00:20:02,760
And when you're ready to go back to work, just step up into the business center.

375
00:20:04,760 --> 00:20:08,000
ARI's standard 132-inch long sleeper

376
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:11,900
will run you about $70,000.

377
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,500
Maybe that's why less than 1% of truckers currently

378
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:16,300
enjoy the creature comforts they offer.

379
00:20:19,400 --> 00:20:23,480
Whether they accommodate luxury sleepers or more modest rigs,

380
00:20:23,580 --> 00:20:27,780
truck stops continue to sprout up across America.

381
00:20:27,880 --> 00:20:31,060
And it's no easy task to create the supersized equipment

382
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:32,520
that keeps them trucking along.

383
00:20:34,760 --> 00:20:39,360
If rigs idled at all 272,000 truck stop parking spaces

384
00:20:39,460 --> 00:20:41,560
across the nation, their emissions

385
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:44,400
would amount to more than 11 million tons of pollutants

386
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:46,300
each year.

387
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:49,400
Truck stops will return on Modern Marvels.

388
00:21:00,020 --> 00:21:02,860
Every night, more than 250,000 truckers

389
00:21:02,861 --> 00:21:05,360
pull into truck stops for refuge.

390
00:21:05,460 --> 00:21:06,960
And every one of them needs a place

391
00:21:07,060 --> 00:21:10,160
to tuck in his rig for the night.

392
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:12,200
But there's a problem.

393
00:21:12,300 --> 00:21:14,200
Truck stops in many US states don't

394
00:21:14,300 --> 00:21:16,600
have enough parking space.

395
00:21:16,700 --> 00:21:20,520
So engineers are busy at work developing new sites.

396
00:21:20,620 --> 00:21:23,220
They're built from below the ground up,

397
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:25,220
beginning with the most indispensable assets

398
00:21:25,320 --> 00:21:28,520
of any truck stop, the diesel tanks.

399
00:21:28,620 --> 00:21:30,820
Here on my left is an eight-foot diameter,

400
00:21:30,821 --> 00:21:34,820
10,000-gallon fuel storage tank, typical of what you would find

401
00:21:34,920 --> 00:21:37,560
at your neighborhood gas station.

402
00:21:37,660 --> 00:21:41,460
Compared to that, on my right, is a 30,000-gallon tank

403
00:21:41,560 --> 00:21:43,860
that is typical of a truck stop.

404
00:21:43,960 --> 00:21:46,260
This tank is 20 feet longer in length.

405
00:21:46,360 --> 00:21:48,480
It's 2 feet larger in diameter.

406
00:21:48,580 --> 00:21:50,480
It's a 10-foot diameter tank.

407
00:21:50,580 --> 00:21:52,480
And it's triple the capacity.

408
00:21:54,980 --> 00:21:58,520
At Containment Solutions in Bakersfield, California,

409
00:21:58,521 --> 00:22:02,320
Tank manufacturing fabricators construct these 30,000 gallon

410
00:22:02,420 --> 00:22:06,420
double walled truck stop tanks one piece at a time.

411
00:22:06,520 --> 00:22:09,160
What you see going on behind me is the rotation

412
00:22:09,260 --> 00:22:14,260
of our steel mandrel, which is the basis of tank forming.

413
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:20,680
We apply resin, glass fibers, and treated silica

414
00:22:20,780 --> 00:22:24,680
to build a composite laminate that is formed on this mold

415
00:22:24,780 --> 00:22:28,520
in multiple passes.

416
00:22:28,521 --> 00:22:32,020
Next, fabricators add plastic reinforcing ribs to the mold.

417
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:38,560
Wrapped with the same fiberglass materials,

418
00:22:38,660 --> 00:22:40,940
these ribs give the tank all the strength it needs

419
00:22:41,040 --> 00:22:42,460
for its long life underground.

420
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:47,280
After the tank sections are cured,

421
00:22:47,380 --> 00:22:52,720
workers pull them from the mold, seal them together,

422
00:22:52,820 --> 00:22:57,220
and pressure test them to ensure that they won't leak fuel.

423
00:22:57,221 --> 00:23:00,460
Any leak could prove environmentally disastrous,

424
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:03,760
since the tanks of a typical truck stop dispense a whopping

425
00:23:03,860 --> 00:23:08,860
1 million gallons of diesel over the course of a month.

426
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:12,260
And the pumps tapping into them fuel four times faster than

427
00:23:12,360 --> 00:23:13,800
those at a neighborhood gas station.

428
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:20,280
In a kind of mechanical symbiosis,

429
00:23:20,380 --> 00:23:22,880
completed tanks make their way to the truck stop

430
00:23:22,980 --> 00:23:24,280
on the beds of trucks.

431
00:23:27,220 --> 00:23:31,220
Once they're buried, the tanks are far from out of mind.

432
00:23:31,221 --> 00:23:34,220
Engineers can keep tabs on them using a monitoring system

433
00:23:34,221 --> 00:23:37,220
linked to this panel installed on the surface.

434
00:23:37,221 --> 00:23:41,220
What this device is, is called an automatic tank gauge.

435
00:23:41,221 --> 00:23:44,220
And what it has is a series of probes and sensors

436
00:23:44,221 --> 00:23:47,220
that are in various portions of the underground storage tank

437
00:23:47,221 --> 00:23:49,220
system. Every tank has a probe in it

438
00:23:49,221 --> 00:23:52,220
that measures the inventory levels in the tank.

439
00:23:52,221 --> 00:23:54,220
The probe is basically an aluminum tube

440
00:23:54,221 --> 00:23:56,220
that is the entire diameter of the tank.

441
00:23:56,221 --> 00:23:59,820
of the tank, and they have a float that moves up and down

442
00:23:59,920 --> 00:24:02,120
on the probe.

443
00:24:02,220 --> 00:24:04,260
The system also has sensors installed

444
00:24:04,360 --> 00:24:06,160
within the double wall containment

445
00:24:06,260 --> 00:24:10,860
that detect any liquid leaking from the interior tank.

446
00:24:10,960 --> 00:24:13,600
The probes and sensors are hardwired to the console

447
00:24:13,700 --> 00:24:17,480
that analyzes and records the data.

448
00:24:17,580 --> 00:24:21,040
If the system senses a leak, an alarm will direct technicians

449
00:24:21,140 --> 00:24:24,120
to the problem.

450
00:24:24,121 --> 00:24:27,820
In the past, maybe the operator would go out once a day

451
00:24:27,920 --> 00:24:30,560
and stick the tank with a tank stick.

452
00:24:30,660 --> 00:24:32,720
Now it's all done electronically.

453
00:24:32,820 --> 00:24:35,660
This measures the tank level down to a hundredths of an inch.

454
00:24:38,460 --> 00:24:40,260
On the pavement above the fuel tanks,

455
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:43,880
truck stop designers face another problem,

456
00:24:43,980 --> 00:24:45,580
making sure the swarm of big rigs

457
00:24:45,680 --> 00:24:49,480
can get in and out of the area efficiently.

458
00:24:49,580 --> 00:24:52,780
Like a parking lot for cars, truck stop parking lots

459
00:24:52,781 --> 00:24:56,780
into parking spaces and drive aisles.

460
00:24:56,880 --> 00:25:00,120
But the drive aisles are more than double the width.

461
00:25:00,220 --> 00:25:02,720
As much as they'd like to, truck stop designers

462
00:25:02,820 --> 00:25:05,120
can't make the aisles much wider than that,

463
00:25:05,220 --> 00:25:09,160
because they have to optimize the available space.

464
00:25:09,260 --> 00:25:11,500
The trick to solving the problem is to understand

465
00:25:11,600 --> 00:25:13,800
a big rig's off-tracking or turning ability.

466
00:25:17,300 --> 00:25:21,240
When a car makes a turn, it follows only one path.

467
00:25:21,241 --> 00:25:23,140
But when a tractor-trailer turns,

468
00:25:23,240 --> 00:25:25,880
the front wheels of the tractor follow one path,

469
00:25:25,980 --> 00:25:27,480
and the rear wheels of the trailer

470
00:25:27,580 --> 00:25:29,980
take a completely different path toward the inside

471
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:32,580
of the turn.

472
00:25:32,680 --> 00:25:36,280
The distance between these two paths, or the off-tracking,

473
00:25:36,380 --> 00:25:38,720
varies depending on the angle of the turn,

474
00:25:38,820 --> 00:25:43,700
the length of the trailer, and the speed of the truck.

475
00:25:43,800 --> 00:25:45,460
Knowing this, truck stop designers

476
00:25:45,560 --> 00:25:48,360
are able to maximize the parking lot space.

477
00:25:48,460 --> 00:25:51,240
If they can't park, they can't use the facility.

478
00:25:51,241 --> 00:25:54,280
And what's very important, once that driver comes on,

479
00:25:54,380 --> 00:25:56,980
they're going to stay at a location for half a day.

480
00:26:00,880 --> 00:26:02,720
That's because federal law specifies

481
00:26:02,820 --> 00:26:05,860
mandatory rest periods for truckers.

482
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:08,320
After driving 11 hours, truck drivers

483
00:26:08,420 --> 00:26:10,820
must pull off the road and rest their weary eyes

484
00:26:10,920 --> 00:26:12,320
for 10 consecutive hours.

485
00:26:16,060 --> 00:26:18,760
Few truck stops come better equipped to meet their needs

486
00:26:18,761 --> 00:26:21,760
than Travel Centers of America, or TA.

487
00:26:24,660 --> 00:26:26,660
We start with very large properties.

488
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:31,100
A typical TA is about 25 acres.

489
00:26:31,200 --> 00:26:34,300
We take that survey and we put it onto a CAD,

490
00:26:34,400 --> 00:26:36,520
a computer-aided design, and we're

491
00:26:36,620 --> 00:26:41,220
able to maximize all parts of the location.

492
00:26:41,320 --> 00:26:44,560
During the planning of this TA in Lodi, Ohio,

493
00:26:44,660 --> 00:26:47,320
designers considered every detail.

494
00:26:47,321 --> 00:26:48,980
The number one thing that comes up

495
00:26:49,080 --> 00:26:53,420
any time we ask the drivers is, just give me a nice, clean,

496
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:56,960
well-designed shower and restroom.

497
00:26:57,060 --> 00:27:00,360
And these real marble showers are not only high class,

498
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:02,060
but also high tech.

499
00:27:02,160 --> 00:27:06,480
Driver number 144 shower number seven is now ready.

500
00:27:06,580 --> 00:27:08,280
In the not so old days, drivers

501
00:27:08,380 --> 00:27:12,020
would have to line up to grab a Spritz.

502
00:27:12,120 --> 00:27:14,120
Now they can order a shower in advance

503
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:17,320
at the same place they fuel up.

504
00:27:17,321 --> 00:27:20,160
And with the purchase of 50 gallons of diesel,

505
00:27:20,260 --> 00:27:21,960
the shower's on the house.

506
00:27:24,120 --> 00:27:26,660
The customer's been on the road for many, many hours.

507
00:27:26,760 --> 00:27:30,560
All they want to do is park their truck and be refreshed.

508
00:27:30,660 --> 00:27:33,900
And to be able to take care of that driver very, very quickly,

509
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,380
that's important.

510
00:27:36,480 --> 00:27:40,080
TA has been innovating for 35 years.

511
00:27:40,180 --> 00:27:42,620
The company began as Truck Stops of America

512
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:46,020
in the early 1970s, as the mystique of the trucker

513
00:27:46,021 --> 00:27:49,120
began to grow.

514
00:27:49,220 --> 00:27:52,560
Truckers were seen as the last American cowboys.

515
00:27:52,660 --> 00:27:54,820
They were these loners out on the road,

516
00:27:54,920 --> 00:28:00,260
and truck stops were where you could mingle with them.

517
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:02,200
As more and more motorists started

518
00:28:02,300 --> 00:28:04,340
pulling off the interstates to check out the trucker's

519
00:28:04,440 --> 00:28:08,480
lifestyle, truck stop owners realized they had been missing

